{% extends "_base.j2" %}
{% block main %}
<header class="scenarios-header">
  <div>
    <span class="eyebrow">Learn · generation assets</span>
    <h1>Generation grammars and mutation tokens</h1>
    <p class="page-hero">These are <strong>machine-readable generation assets</strong>, not the human glossary. DWARF inventories {{ rows|length }} structure/token sets with {{ token_count }} parsed mutation tokens.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="definition-actions">
    <a class="cta cta--ghost" href="/operate/grammars">Browse definitions</a>
    <a class="cta cta--ghost" href="/api/grammars/export" download>Download all</a>
  </div>
</header>

<section aria-labelledby="grammar-boundaries-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow">Boundaries</span>
  <h2 id="grammar-boundaries-heading">Three related assets, three different jobs</h2>
  <div class="learn-tile-grid">
    <article class="learn-tile"><span class="learn-tile__eyebrow">Specification and valid generation</span><h3 class="learn-tile__title">CDDL + Cuddle</h3><p>CDDL describes valid CBOR data. Cuddle can validate that schema and generate structure-conforming CBOR seeds outside the live fuzz loop. DWARF retains generated bytes in corpora; Cuddle is not a required node runtime.</p></article>
    <article class="learn-tile"><span class="learn-tile__eyebrow">Descriptive target metadata</span><h3 class="learn-tile__title"><code>structure.json</code></h3><p>Records the target, real decoder entrypoint, and a bounded description of the outer input shape. In the current engine it documents and validates the shape; it is not by itself an executable grammar or protocol state machine.</p></article>
    <article class="learn-tile"><span class="learn-tile__eyebrow">Mutation guidance</span><h3 class="learn-tile__title"><code>dict.txt</code></h3><p>Contains quoted byte strings that bias mutations toward useful delimiters, message identifiers, CBOR prefixes, and constants. Mutation tokens improve reachability; they neither define valid input nor prove protocol correctness.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="grammar-engines-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow">Execution engines</span>
  <h2 id="grammar-engines-heading">The tools are not interchangeable</h2>
  <div class="engine-flow" aria-label="Generation asset flow">
    <div><strong>CDDL / Cuddle</strong><span>optional valid-seed generation</span></div>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div><strong>Corpus bytes</strong><span>real inputs retained by DWARF</span></div>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div><strong>Fuzzer + harness</strong><span>mutates bytes and reaches a real target</span></div>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div><strong>Evidence</strong><span>outcome, crash, coverage, or assertion</span></div>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>cargo-fuzz</strong> drives Rust fuzz targets with <strong>libFuzzer</strong>; these shipped <code>dict.txt</code> files use its quoted-byte dictionary form.</li>
    <li><strong>AFL++</strong> is a separate coverage-guided engine used by other DWARF parser and harness paths. A compatible-looking token file does not make cargo-fuzz and AFL++ the same engine.</li>
    <li><strong>AFLNet</strong> is a separate state-aware network-fuzzing path. A token dictionary is not a full protocol transcript, agency model, or session-state machine.</li>
    <li><strong>DWARF campaigns and scenarios</strong> select the real target, corpus, primitive, and assertions. A shipped cargo-fuzz dictionary is auto-discovered only when its directory name matches the fuzz-directory basename; runtime clones require an explicit <code>--dict-path</code> or supported primitive <code>dict_path</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="grammar-schema-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow">Schema</span>
  <h2 id="grammar-schema-heading"><code>generation-structure.schema.json</code></h2>
  <div class="responsive-table"><table class="config-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>format</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Encoding family. The current shipped set supports <code>cbor</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>target</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Stable fuzz-target declaration.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>decoder_entrypoint</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Real implementation or harness function reached by the target.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>shape</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Descriptive object with at least a <code>type</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>major_types</code>, <code>cbor_tokens</code>, <code>sample_sources</code></td><td>no</td><td>Explanatory groupings and source provenance.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p><code>dict.txt</code> accepts blank lines, <code>#</code> comments, optional token names, quoted literals, and finite byte escapes such as <code>\x82</code>. Invalid lines remain visible as diagnostics. Content is never evaluated.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="grammar-lifecycle-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow">Lifecycle</span>
  <h2 id="grammar-lifecycle-heading">Inspect → clone → edit → validate → reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Inspect the immutable definition and exact token bytes.</li>
    <li>Clone both files into the explicitly configured runtime grammar root.</li>
    <li>Edit with the structured builder or raw JSON/text mode.</li>
    <li>Pass schema and finite dictionary validation before either file is written.</li>
    <li>Select the runtime asset explicitly with <code>cargo_fuzz_campaign.py --dict-path</code> or the <code>dict_path</code> parameter of a supported runtime campaign primitive. Cloning alone does not activate it.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="grammar-inventory-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow">Current inventory</span>
  <h2 id="grammar-inventory-heading">Individual structure/token sets</h2>
  <div class="learn-tile-grid">
    {% for row in rows %}<a class="learn-tile" href="/operate/grammars/{{ row.id }}"><span class="learn-tile__eyebrow">{{ row.root_kind }} · {{ row.status }}</span><h3 class="learn-tile__title"><code>{{ row.label }}</code></h3><p class="learn-tile__sub">{{ row.target or 'target unavailable' }} · {{ row.token_count }} tokens</p></a>{% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="grammar-examples-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow">Examples</span>
  <h2 id="grammar-examples-heading">Supported operator actions</h2>
  {% for example in examples %}<article class="example-block"><h3>{{ example.title }}</h3><p>{{ example.description }}</p><pre><code>{{ example.example }}</code></pre></article>{% endfor %}
</section>
{% endblock %}
